>>__ load "simpExtLib";
>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `mk_simpset`

``` hol4
simpExtLib.mk_simpset : simpExtLib.ssfrag list -> simpExtLib.simpset
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Constructs an extension simpset from a list of fragments.

`mk_simpset frags` adds `frags`, in order, to `simpExtLib.empty_ss`. As
with `simpLib.mk_simpset`, the order matters for rewrite makers and
congruence rules.

`simpExtLib` is an opt-in simplifier that adds solvers, loopers, split
rules, configurable side-condition handling and child-first traversal to
the ordinary simplifier of `simpLib`. Its `ssfrag` and `simpset` types are
distinct from those of `simpLib`:

- `simpExtLib.lift_ssfrag` turns an ordinary fragment into an extension
  fragment, and `simpExtLib.lift_simpset` an ordinary simpset into an
  extension simpset; for example,
  `simpExtLib.lift_simpset boolSimps.bool_ss`. A simpset that is extended
  over time, such as `bossLib.srw_ss()`, must be lifted again to see the
  additions.
- Decision procedures in extension fragments have type
  `TraverseExt.reducer`; `TraverseExt.lift` turns an ordinary
  `Traverse.reducer` into one.
- Fragments registered with `simpExtLib.register_frag` are found by
  `simpExtLib.SF`, as are fragments registered with
  `simpLib.register_frag`, unless an extension fragment of the same name
  is registered. The ordinary simplifier does not see extension
  registrations.

`simpExtLib.ssfrags_of ss` gives only the fragments of `ss`: a simpset
rebuilt from them loses the deletions, exclusions, limit and settings of
`ss`.

### Failure

Fails if adding a fragment fails.

### Example

``` hol4
>>_ val ss = simpExtLib.mk_simpset
     [simpExtLib.lift_ssfrag pureSimps.PURE_ss,
      simpExtLib.rewrites [boolTheory.AND_CLAUSES]];
>> simpExtLib.SIMP_CONV ss [] “T ∧ p”;
```

